--TEST--
Core\Cli::multiSelect answers an array<T> rather than a T, and is the one prompt with no default — ADR 0086 § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// This case runs with every standard stream redirected, which is § 4's "no
// controlling terminal". The four prompts beside this one answer a `default`
// there; § 4's table gives `multiSelect` none, because an empty line already
// names the empty set and that is the only thing a default would have been for.
array<string> $choices = ["alpha", "beta"];
try {
    array<string> $picked = Core\Cli::multiSelect("Pick some", $choices);
    echo Core\Arr::count($picked), "\n";
} catch (Core\Cli\NotInteractive $noTerminal) {
    echo $noTerminal->message, "\n";
}

// The binding above is the other half of this case, and it is checked at
// compile time rather than printed: `select<T>` answers `T` and `multiSelect<T>`
// answers `array<T>`, so a case that binds one to an `array<string>` and still
// compiles has pinned which of the two this member is. `$choices` is written as
// a typed local rather than inline for ADR 0007 § 2's reason — an array literal
// in a generic position has nothing to be placed against, so `T` would come out
// `mixed` here exactly as it does for `select`.
try {
    string $one = Core\Cli::select("Pick one", $choices);
    echo $one, "\n";
} catch (Core\Cli\NotInteractive $noChoice) {
    echo "select answers one\n";
}

// An empty list is refused before the terminal is consulted at all, exactly as
// `select`'s is: there is nothing that could be chosen either way, so it is a
// bug in the program rather than a question about the environment.
array<string> $empty = [];
try {
    Core\Cli::multiSelect("Pick some", $empty);
} catch (LogicError $nothingToPick) {
    echo $nothingToPick->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
no answer for Core\Cli::multiSelect: there is no controlling terminal
select answers one
Core\Cli::multiSelect was given nothing to choose between
